think the entire  country is behind him he's one of those  inspirational leaders that sometimes  emerges in wartime  i hope he's taking great care of his own  safety  and i think you know we should be giving  him all the advice and support we can in  that  it's i think really really important for  ukraine that he continues as the  not just the symbolic head but the  manifestation of their extraordinary  national will to resist  and how worried should we be about  waiving um visas and the security issues  that that might cause in the united  kingdom which is what we're being told  is one of the problems of the log jam of  visa applications  i think we have to put the two parts of  our conversation together i mean we  can't react with horror  at the indiscriminate attacks on  civilians and then immediately turn  around and say we're going to impose an  awful lot of bureaucratic requirements  on those fleeing this terror and making  it extremely difficult for them to come  to this country  the eu are accepting in i think it's now  over 1.5 million people on the basis of  temporary  residence permits  no doubt with checks to come later if  they're still in the eu in some months  time i don't see that there's any  security risk from allowing in women and  children  from ukraine family members  joining others in this country so i  would be inclined to look to waive those  requirements particularly for women and  children and if possible for all  ukrainians perhaps on the basis of a  temporary right to stay for six months  say during which checks could be  undertaken if they want to stay in this  country  look at these pictures as we're speaking  to you my lord these little people with  snow falling they're absolutely freezing  up to two million people have fled the  country they don't know where they're  going to go it's very difficult not to  be emotional about it isn't it  it's difficult not to be emotional about  it and it's difficult not to be saying  to our government you have to do better  to allow those who want to come to this  country who's got families here to make  it possible for them to do so i don't  think we are in normal times where  normal levels of security checks and  biometric checks can be enforced these  people are desperate they're fleeing the  sort of horror we've just seen in mario  paul and we need to find the  humanitarian side of our nature i think  as other european countries are doing to  be a welcoming generous haven for these  poor people all our hearts go out to  them i'm sure this morning  did you think lord ricketts my lord that  that we would see  something like this again in your  lifetime in europe the biggest  movement of people since the second  world war no i really didn't and i spent  40 years as a diplomat trying to  strengthen that set of international  rules which was meant to ensure that  this never happened again they were  brought in after the second world war  which is the last time we saw a horror  on this scale  i lived through the 1990s and the ethnic  cleansing in former yugoslavia which was  terrible but much more limited than this  i really never thought we'd get back to  full-scale war against the civilian  population in a european country it is a  really desperately sad setback to the  darkest days of the 20th century and the  whole world needs to come together to  put the pressure on russia to stop this  awful assault  and a final thought from you my lord if  you will  those that are serving military that are  so  uh overwhelmed by what they see  giving up their posts and going to  ukraine the armed forces minister not at  all happy about that what would you say  well  i wouldn't encourage people to do that i  think we all understand the sense of  incredible exasperation frustration and  wish to do something but remember this  is now a war zone with no laws the  russians are no respecters of the laws  of war um if anyone fell into russian  hands i hate to think what happened to  them i think we should be helping the  ukrainians in every way through material  through from weapons to economic support  through humanitarian support  i would not advise people to go to this  war zone the british government cannot  help anyone who gets into trouble once  they're in ukraine  we appreciate you very much taking the  time to speak to us on the program this  morning my lord thank you  thank you  still to come in the next hour of the  program we'll have more reaction to the  recent developments in ukraine as the  armed forces minister james heapey says  what we're seeing on our television  screens is war crimes by russia in  addition to that he warned british  serving military personnel not to head  to ukraine and in his words have come up  with selfies  he said that they will be in an awful  lot of trouble what does ed miliband  think about that we'll be asking him in  just a moment here on scania  [Music]  [Music]  what happened  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

